<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Racibórz</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ratibor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Racibórz (German: Ratibor) is a town in the Silesian Voivodeship of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, on the upper Oder. From the late 12th century it was a seat of the Piast dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-silesia">Upper Silesia</a> and later the capital of the separate Duchy of Racibórz. After 1742 it belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and it returned to Poland in 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raciborz.jpg" alt="Racibórz" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hons084 · CC BY-SA 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ko%C5%9Bci%C3%B3%C5%82_%C5%9Bw._Ducha_(obecnie_muzeum_miejskie)_w_Raciborzu_6.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
